<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>destination</key>
	<string>export</string>
	<key>generateAppStoreInformation</key>
	<false/>
	<key>manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Same method string as the iOS ExportOptions.plist: `app-store-connect`
	     is what `xcodebuild -help` lists for Xcode 26 ("Additional options
	     include app-store (deprecated: use app-store-connect)"). The method
	     does not name a platform — the *archive* does. Point this file at a
	     Mac Catalyst archive and the same method produces the macOS product. -->
	<key>method</key>
	<string>app-store-connect</string>
	<!-- One entry per MAIN EXECUTABLE in the archive, and both of these are
	     distinct App IDs needing their own macOS-platform profile: a profile is
	     platform-scoped, and the iOS pair ("TimeConverter App Store",
	     "TimeConverter Controls App Store") report platforms
	     "visionOS, watchOS, and iOS" — xcodebuild refuses them for platform
	     "macOS" outright. Neither of the names below exists in the portal yet;
	     see docs/testflight-setup-checklist.md "macOS train".

	     Bundle ids are the SAME as iOS — a Catalyst app distributed through the
	     App Store keeps its iOS bundle id, with no `maccatalyst.` prefix.

	     Deliberately absent: dev.thisolivier.timeconverter.menubar. That is a
	     traditional loadable .bundle, not a main executable — it has no
	     profile and no entitlements by design (project.yml and
	     TimeConverterMenuBar/readme.md explain why), so there is nothing to
	     map. Adding it here would mean minting a profile for it, and an
	     embedded profile on a secondary bundle is precisely what produces
	     ITMS-90283. -->
	<key>provisioningProfiles</key>
	<dict>
		<key>dev.thisolivier.timeconverter</key>
		<string>TimeConverter Mac App Store</string>
		<key>dev.thisolivier.timeconverter.controls</key>
		<string>TimeConverter Controls Mac App Store</string>
	</dict>
	<key>signingCertificate</key>
	<string>Apple Distribution</string>
	<key>signingStyle</key>
	<string>manual</string>
	<key>stripSwiftSymbols</key>
	<true/>
	<key>teamID</key>
	<string>CNHB9B5GX6</string>
	<key>testFlightInternalTestingOnly</key>
	<false/>
	<key>uploadSymbols</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- The macOS App Store product is an *installer package*, not an app
	     bundle: `man 7 altool` says "To upload your app binary files (.ipa or
	     .pkg)", Xcode Help's "Archive export files" says "for macOS apps, this
	     is an installer package (with a .pkg file extension)", and DTS is
	     blunt about it — "A Mac App Store app must be submitted as an installer
	     package" (forums 701581). So this export signs a .pkg, and a .pkg is
	     signed by an *installer* identity, which is a different certificate
	     type from a code-signing one: "Installer certificates have different
	     OIDs than code signing certificates. They are not interchangeable in
	     any way" (DTS, forums 740423).
	     `signingCertificate` above therefore does NOT cover it.

	     This is the one brand-new certificate the macOS train needs, and this
	     machine does not have it yet: `security find-identity -v` shows only
	     Apple Distribution and Apple Development, and under
	     `-p macappstore` both report "Missing required extension".
	     "Mac Installer Distribution" is an automatic selector — Xcode picks the
	     newest installed certificate of that type — and is also what this key
	     would default to; it is spelled out because being told
	     "No signing certificate 'Mac Installer Distribution' found" by
	     `-exportArchive` is otherwise mystifying. `scripts/ship-mac.sh`
	     preflights for it so the failure arrives before the archive, not after. -->
	<key>installerSigningCertificate</key>
	<string>Mac Installer Distribution</string>
</dict>
</plist>
